      "id": 1,
      "text": "Slabtown got its name in the 1870s when a lumber mill on Northrup Street opened, and its discarded log edges became cheap heating fuel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/ci_fac/95/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1905 Lewis and Clark Centennial Exposition, along with shipbuilding and manufacturing, helped shape Slabtown’s early 20th‑century identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/ci_fac/95/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Slabtown is a mixed‑use neighborhood where you can live, work, dine, shop, and play—all within a few blocks of downtown Portland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://getaroundslabtown.org/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, Slabtown is a densely populated residential neighborhood, with many small shops and restaurants and an industrial area on its northern border.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/ci_fac/95/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Slabtown sits between Portland’s two trendiest Northwest neighborhoods, the Pearl and Nob Hill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slabtown%2C_Portland%2C_Oregon",
